{"p":"/scripting/attributes","t":"Properties and attributes","d":"How to use scripts to manipulate object properties and attributes.","h":"<p>Making games interactive often means manipulating object properties and attributes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Properties are part of the object class. For example, the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/BasePart/BasePart.Anchored\"><code>BasePart.Anchored</code></a> property controls physics for the part. In a track and field game, you might want to anchor a discus or javelin the instant it lands so that players have a visual indicator of how far it traveled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Attributes are essentially custom properties that you define. For example, the <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/resources/plant-reference-project\">Plant</a> reference project uses attributes to set the purchase price for seeds and the maximum plant size that a pot can hold.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Replication order</h2>\n<p>Before you begin retrieving and manipulating objects, you must have an understanding of replication order.</p>\n<p>The Roblox Engine doesn't guarantee the order in which objects (and changes to objects) are replicated from the server to the client, which makes the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:WaitForChild\"><code>Instance:WaitForChild()</code></a> method essential for accessing objects in client scripts, particularly objects in the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Workspace\"><code>Workspace</code></a>.</p>\n<p>For example, if a server script changes a property of some instance in the Workspace and then calls <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/RemoteEvent/RemoteEvent:FireAllClients\"><code>RemoteEvent:FireAllClients()</code></a>, the property change might replicate to the client before or after <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/RemoteEvent/RemoteEvent.OnClientEvent\"><code>RemoteEvent.OnClientEvent</code></a> fires. Use the available methods and events to <a href=\"#detect-changes\">detect changes</a> rather than assuming a change has replicated. Changes of the same type, such as two attribute changes, generally <strong>do</strong> arrive in order.</p>\n<p>When clients first launch a game, some aspects of replication and script execution are predictable:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The client loads the contents of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ReplicatedFirst\"><code>ReplicatedFirst</code></a>, such as a loading screen, assets, and scripts.</li>\n<li><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/LocalScript\"><code>LocalScripts</code></a> (and <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Script\"><code>Scripts</code></a> with a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Script/Script.RunContext\"><code>RunContext</code></a> of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/RunContext/RunContext.Client\"><code>Client</code></a>) in <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ReplicatedFirst\"><code>ReplicatedFirst</code></a> run. These scripts can safely get objects from <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ReplicatedFirst\"><code>ReplicatedFirst</code></a> without using <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:WaitForChild\"><code>Instance:WaitForChild()</code></a>:</li>\n</ol>\n<p><code>lua\n   -- Safe\n   local ReplicatedFirst = game:GetService(\"ReplicatedFirst\")\n   local LoadingScreen = require(ReplicatedFirst.LoadingScreen)</code></p>\n<p>These scripts <strong>can't</strong> safely get objects from other <a href=\"#/docs/scripting/services\">services</a>, because they might not have loaded yet:</p>\n<p><code>lua\n   -- Not safe\n   local ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService(\"ReplicatedStorage\")\n   local PickupManager = require(ReplicatedStorage.PickupManager)</code></p>\n<p>You <strong>can</strong> use <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:WaitForChild\"><code>WaitForChild()</code></a> in these scripts to get objects from other services, but doing so negates the benefits of using <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ReplicatedFirst\"><code>ReplicatedFirst</code></a>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>The client continues loading the rest of the game.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When it finishes, the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/DataModel/DataModel.Loaded\"><code>Loaded</code></a> event fires and <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/DataModel/DataModel:IsLoaded\"><code>IsLoaded()</code></a> returns true.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/LocalScript\"><code>LocalScripts</code></a> in <code>Players.[Player].PlayerScripts</code> (copied from <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/StarterPlayerScripts\"><code>StarterPlayerScripts</code></a>) run, as well as client <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Script\"><code>Scripts</code></a> in <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ReplicatedStorage\"><code>ReplicatedStorage</code></a>. These scripts can safely get objects from <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ReplicatedStorage\"><code>ReplicatedStorage</code></a> without using <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:WaitForChild\"><code>WaitForChild()</code></a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The player's <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Player/Player.Character\"><code>Character</code></a> model spawns in the game.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/LocalScript\"><code>LocalScripts</code></a> in <code>Workspace.[Character]</code> (copied from <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/StarterCharacterScripts\"><code>StarterCharacterScripts</code></a>) run.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If your game uses <a href=\"#/docs/workspace/streaming\">instance streaming</a> (<a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Workspace/Workspace.StreamingEnabled\"><code>Workspace.StreamingEnabled</code></a>), some or most objects might not have loaded into the workspace, so using <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:WaitForChild\"><code>WaitForChild()</code></a> to access workspace objects becomes an even more important safety measure. In particular, see <a href=\"#/docs/workspace/streaming\">stream in</a> and <a href=\"#/docs/workspace/streaming\">per-model streaming controls</a> for additional information on loading and tuning streaming behavior.</p>\n<h2>Get objects</h2>\n<p>The first step to modifying object properties and attributes is to get a reference to the object. The simplest solution is to make the script a child of the object in the <strong>Explorer</strong> and use <code>script.Parent</code> to reference the object.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A script parented to a model in the Explorer.\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local sign = script.Parent\n</code></pre>\n<p>The more universal solution is to get the object from a <a href=\"#/docs/scripting/services\">service</a> using methods like <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:FindFirstChild\"><code>Instance:FindFirstChild()</code></a> or <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:WaitForChild\"><code>Instance:WaitForChild()</code></a>.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A Model within a Folder in ReplicatedStorage.\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService(&quot;ReplicatedStorage&quot;)\n\nlocal signsFolder = ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(&quot;Signs&quot;)\nlocal sign = signsFolder:WaitForChild(&quot;InteractiveSign&quot;)\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Modify properties</h2>\n<p>Properties are straightforward to access — just use a <code>.</code> after the object reference&nbsp;— although if you're working with a model, you might need to choose an individual part rather than the model itself.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A Model within ReplicatedStorage.\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService(&quot;ReplicatedStorage&quot;)\nlocal chair = ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(&quot;Chair&quot;)\n\nchair.LeftArmRest.Size = Vector3.new(10, 1, 10)\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Create attributes</h2>\n<p>Although you can create attributes programmatically, the more common solution is to create them with default values in the Studio user interface. Then you can use scripts to modify their values in response to player actions.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"A script within a folder in ReplicatedStorage.\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<p>For information on creating attributes in Studio, see <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/studio/properties#instance-attributes\">instance attributes</a>.</p>\n<h2>Set attributes</h2>\n<p>To modify an attribute's value, call <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:SetAttribute\"><code>Instance:SetAttribute()</code></a> with a name and value.</p>\n<p><strong>Create or Modify Attribute</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local cabbage = script.Parent\n\ncabbage:SetAttribute(&quot;Harvestable&quot;, true)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If the attribute doesn't already exist, this method creates it.</p>\n<h2>Get attribute values</h2>\n<p>To get the value of one existing attribute, call <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:GetAttribute\"><code>Instance:GetAttribute()</code></a> on the instance.</p>\n<p><strong>Get Attribute Value</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local cabbage = script.Parent\n\ncabbage:SetAttribute(&quot;Harvestable&quot;, true)\n\nlocal isHarvestable = cabbage:GetAttribute(&quot;Harvestable&quot;)\nprint(isHarvestable) --&gt; true\n</code></pre>\n<p>Similarly, you can get all attributes by calling <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:GetAttributes\"><code>Instance:GetAttributes()</code></a>. This method returns a dictionary of key-value pairs.</p>\n<p><strong>Get All Attributes</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local cabbage = script.Parent\n\nlocal cabbageAttributes = cabbage:GetAttributes()\n\nprint(cabbageAttributes.GrowthRate) --&gt; 2\n\nfor k, v in cabbageAttributes do\n    print(k, v)\nend\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Delete attributes</h2>\n<p>To delete an attribute, set its value to <code>nil</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Delete Attribute</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local cabbage = script.Parent\n\ncabbage:SetAttribute(&quot;GrowthRate&quot;, nil)\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Detect changes</h2>\n<p>There are several ways to listen for changes to properties and attributes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance.AttributeChanged\"><code>Instance.AttributeChanged</code></a> event listens for changes to any attribute and passes the name of the changed attribute as a parameter.</li>\n<li>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:GetPropertyChangedSignal\"><code>Instance:GetPropertyChangedSignal()</code></a> method lets you listen for changes to one property and passes no parameters.</li>\n<li>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:GetAttributeChangedSignal\"><code>Instance:GetAttributeChangedSignal()</code></a> method lets you listen for changes to one attribute and passes no parameters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Due to the minimal information that these events and methods pass as parameters, all of them are a good fit for anonymous functions, particularly <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:GetPropertyChangedSignal\"><code>Instance:GetPropertyChangedSignal()</code></a> and <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Instance/Instance:GetAttributeChangedSignal\"><code>Instance:GetAttributeChangedSignal()</code></a>. To learn more about anonymous functions and working with events, see <a href=\"#/docs/scripting/events\">Events</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Listen for Changes</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-lua\">local cabbage = script.Parent\n\n-- Local functions\nlocal function onAnyPropertyChange(property)\n    -- Ignore changes to attributes\n    if property ~= &quot;Attributes&quot; then\n        print(property) --&gt; Name\n        print(cabbage[property]) --&gt; Cabbage1\n    end\nend\n\nlocal function onAnyAttributeChange(attribute)\n    print(attribute) --&gt; Grow, GrowthRate\n    print(cabbage:GetAttribute(attribute)) --&gt; false, 3\nend\n\n-- Listen for changes and connect to local functions\ncabbage.Changed:Connect(onAnyPropertyChange)\ncabbage.AttributeChanged:Connect(onAnyAttributeChange)\n\n-- Listen for changes and connect to anonymous functions\ncabbage:GetPropertyChangedSignal(&quot;Name&quot;):Connect(function()\n    print(cabbage.Name) --&gt; Cabbage1\nend)\ncabbage:GetAttributeChangedSignal(&quot;GrowthRate&quot;):Connect(function()\n    print(cabbage:GetAttribute(&quot;GrowthRate&quot;)) --&gt; 3\nend)\n\n-- Fires Changed and GetPropertyChangedSignal()\ncabbage.Name = &quot;Cabbage1&quot;\n\n-- Fires Changed and AttributeChanged\ncabbage:SetAttribute(&quot;Grow&quot;, false)\n\n-- Fires Changed, AttributeChanged, and GetAttributeChangedSignal()\ncabbage:SetAttribute(&quot;GrowthRate&quot;, 3)\n</code></pre>","kid":{"simple":"Properties are the built-in settings of an object, like a part's `Size` or `Color`. Attributes are your own custom settings you add, like `Price = 50` or `Harvestable = true`. This page shows how to find objects, change their properties, add and read attributes, and run code when any of them change. It also explains why client scripts should use `WaitForChild`, because things load in an unknown order.","analogy":"Properties are like the facts printed on a trading card (name, color, size). Attributes are like sticky notes you add yourself, such as 'worth 50 coins' or 'can be harvested'.","keyPoints":["Get an object with `script.Parent` if the script is inside it, or with `FindFirstChild` and `WaitForChild` from a service.","Change a property with a dot: `part.Size = Vector3.new(10, 1, 10)`.","`SetAttribute(\"Name\", value)` creates or changes an attribute. Set it to `nil` to delete it.","`GetAttribute(\"Name\")` reads one attribute; `GetAttributes()` gives a table of all of them.","Listen for changes with `Changed` (any property), `AttributeChanged` (any attribute), `GetPropertyChangedSignal(\"Name\")` (one property), or `GetAttributeChangedSignal(\"Name\")` (one attribute).","Roblox does not promise the order things are copied from the server to players, so client scripts should use `WaitForChild` instead of assuming something exists.","On joining, the client loads ReplicatedFirst first, then the rest of the game, then runs PlayerScripts, then spawns the character and runs character scripts.","Changes of the same type, like two attribute changes, usually arrive in order."],"terms":{"property":"A built-in setting on an object, like a part's `Size`, `Color`, or `Anchored`.","attribute":"A custom setting you add to an object yourself, like `GrowthRate = 2`.","replication":"Copying objects and changes from the server to each player's device.","replication order":"The order things arrive on a player's device. It is not guaranteed, so something might not be there yet when your script runs.","reference":"A variable that points to an object so you can work with it, like `local sign = script.Parent`.","WaitForChild":"Waits until a child with that name exists, then gives it to you. Safe for client scripts.","FindFirstChild":"Looks for a child with that name right now. Returns `nil` if it is not there yet.","signal":"Another word for an event you can connect a function to.","anonymous function":"A function with no name, written right inside `:Connect(function() ... end)`.","dictionary":"A table with named keys, like `{ GrowthRate = 2, Harvestable = true }`."},"example":{"title":"A crop that becomes harvestable","place":"Script inside a Part named Cabbage in Workspace","code":"local cabbage = script.Parent\n\n-- Create attributes with starting values\ncabbage:SetAttribute(\"Harvestable\", false)\ncabbage:SetAttribute(\"GrowthRate\", 2)\n\n-- Run code whenever Harvestable changes\ncabbage:GetAttributeChangedSignal(\"Harvestable\"):Connect(function()\n\tif cabbage:GetAttribute(\"Harvestable\") then\n\t\tcabbage.Color = Color3.fromRGB(0, 200, 0) -- turn bright green\n\t\tprint(\"The cabbage is ready!\")\n\tend\nend)\n\n-- Wait a few seconds, then make it harvestable\ntask.wait(10 / cabbage:GetAttribute(\"GrowthRate\")) -- 5 seconds\ncabbage:SetAttribute(\"Harvestable\", true)","explain":["We add two attributes to the cabbage: `Harvestable` and `GrowthRate`.","We listen for changes to just `Harvestable` with `GetAttributeChangedSignal`.","When it becomes true, we change the cabbage's `Color` property to green.","We wait 10 divided by the growth rate (10 / 2 = 5 seconds), then set `Harvestable` to true, which fires our listener."]},"watchOut":["Attribute names are case-sensitive. `GetAttribute(\"growthrate\")` will not find `GrowthRate`.","In client scripts, use `WaitForChild` for objects from the server. Using a dot right away can error if the object has not arrived yet.","`GetPropertyChangedSignal` and `GetAttributeChangedSignal` do not tell you the new value. Read it inside your function.","A Model does not have properties like `Size` or `Color` the way parts do. Pick the part inside the model to change those."]}}